\title{Clamp the elements of an array in place when it is not shared}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{simd}
\tag{example}
\import{base-macros}

\p{A scan that writes. The array is updated through its data pointer only when \code{\verb~|is_unique~} says nobody else can observe the write; otherwise the ordinary copy-on-write path runs.}
\pre{\verb~|
import core::intrinsic::array;
import core::ptr;
import core::simd;
import core::ptr::PtrArithFlag;

pub fn clamp(a: [f32; ?], limit: f32) -> [f32; ?] {
    let len = array::dim(a, 0);
    if core::object::is_unique(a) {
        let base = ptr::as_mut(array::unsafe_data_ptr(a));
        let top = simd::splat<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>(limit);
        let width = simd::lanes<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>();
        let end = while [i = 0] (i < len) {
            let live = simd::mask_first<Simd<[bool; [4]]>>(len - i);
            let at = ptr::advance<f32>(base, i as i64, PtrArithFlag::Inbounds);
            let x = simd::load<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>(at, live, top);
            simd::store(at, top, simd::gt(x, top, live));
            i + width
        };
        a
    } else {
        clamp_copy(a, limit, 0)
    }
}

fn clamp_copy(a: [f32; ?], limit: f32, i: i64) -> [f32; ?] {
    if (i as u64) >= array::dim(a, 0) { a } else {
        if array::get(a, i) > limit { clamp_copy(array::set(a, i, limit), limit, i + 1) }
        else { clamp_copy(a, limit, i + 1) }
    }
}
~}
\ul{
\li{The array stays alive without \code{\verb~|unsafe_inc~} because it is returned: its last use comes after the loop.}
\li{The store is masked by the comparison, so only the elements above the limit are written. No blend is needed.}
\li{\code{\verb~|as_mut~} is where the program takes responsibility for writing through a pointer that was handed out read-only.}
}
\p{Belongs to [SIMD vectors](design-simd-vectors). A fixed-vector form of it runs in the standard library tests, \pr{1006}; the scalable form shown here compiles and has not been executed.}
